 "caveats": [
  "Run only for shutdowns in the 25 countries Cordon's history covers (FCDO's 24 and the US embassies', with Israel and Palestine apart); elsewhere Cordon is not replayed. Both runs, two layers and three, are over the same shutdowns and control days.",
  "Cordon's history: FCDO travel advice (24 countries) and US embassy alerts (23; Iran has no US embassy, so it has no alerts here, which means no data, not no alerts) are dense; State's advisory levels (39 countries) are weak, rebuilt from Wayback captures of State's RSS with 85% of the window in stretches over 7 days between captures.",
  "Canada's and New Zealand's travel advice, which live triage also reads, are not rebuilt; nor are State's departure-posture items. Israel's and Palestine's State levels are unknown (State's RSS never lists Israel).",
  "A counted notice reads tension context even when Watchfloor's day is incomplete; live triage would read the outage incomplete until every layer has data.",
  "Watchfloor's zone items are not replayed, only its country items.",
  "STOP dates a shutdown by the day; each is read at 12:00 UTC on its first day, with Watchfloor's last full day before it.",
  "STOP's 2025 list is mostly shutdowns in conflict and protests, and Myanmar and India are over half of it, so the country rows matter more than the pooled ones.",
  "Most STOP shutdowns are regional; triage reads countries, so a regional shutdown is read as its country.",
  "Each shutdown is compared with its own country's control days. Myanmar has none (its shutdowns, with 7 days either side and open-ended ones taken as 30 days, cover all of 2025), so its 83 shutdowns are in the all-shutdowns shares and left out of the matched comparison.",
  "A replay says how often a documented shutdown would have read each way, and how often any day would have. It is not a test of whether triage finds shutdowns, and no reading is a finding of cause."
 ],
